<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-determinants-inverses-products" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Determinants of Inverses, Products, and Transposes</title>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-det-and-invertibility">
    <title>The Determinant Detects Invertibility</title>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-det-nonzero-iff-invertible">
      <title>Invertibility and the Determinant</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          An <m>n \times n</m> matrix <m>A</m> is invertible (Statement 1) if and only if
          <m>\det A \neq 0</m> (Statement 2). In this case,
          <md>
            \det(A^{-1}) = \frac{1}{\det A}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          <term>Statement 1 <m>\Longrightarrow</m> Statement 2.</term>
          If <m>A_{n \times n}</m> is invertible, then we know that
          <m>\left[ A \mid I \right]</m> can be carried to
          <m>\left[ I \mid A^{-1} \right]</m> by elementary operations. We also know that
          elementary operations are reversible. Hence <m>A</m> can be obtained from
          <m>I</m> by performing a sequence of elementary operations. By
          <xref ref="thm-row-ops-determinant" text="type-global" />, each such operation
          multiplies the determinant by a nonzero factor, so we conclude that
          <m>\det A = \alpha \det I = \alpha</m>, where <m>\alpha \in \R</m> and
          <m>\alpha \neq 0</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>Statement 2 <m>\Longrightarrow</m> Statement 1.</term>
          We show that if Statement 1 is NOT true, then Statement 2 is NOT true. If
          <m>A</m> is not invertible, then <m>A</m> must have fewer than <m>n</m> pivot
          positions. This means the reduced row-echelon matrix will have at least one zero
          on its main diagonal, and since that matrix is upper triangular, its determinant
          is the product of the entries on the main diagonal, and hence <m>\det A = 0</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The fact that <m>\det A^{-1} = \dfrac{1}{\det A}</m> is shown in
          <xref ref="cor-det-inverse" text="type-global" />, after we discuss
          <m>\det(AB) = \det A \det B</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>

    <example xml:id="eg-noninvertible-product">
      <title>True or False?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are two square matrices and <m>A</m> is not invertible,
          then <m>AB</m> is not invertible.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <term>True.</term> We show the contrapositive: if <m>AB</m> is invertible, then
          <m>A</m> is invertible. Note that if <m>AB</m> is invertible, then there exists
          a matrix <m>C</m> such that <m>(AB)C = I</m>, which means <m>A(BC) = I</m>. By
          condition (8) of <xref ref="thm-invertible-matrix-theorem" text="type-global" />,
          <m>A</m> is invertible with <m>A^{-1} = BC</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-det-of-product">
    <title>The Determinant of a Product</title>

    <example xml:id="eg-det-elementary-product">
      <title>True or False?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For any elementary matrix <m>E</m> and square matrix <m>M</m>, we have
          <md>
            \det(EM) = \det(E) \det(M).
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <term>True.</term> We check the three types of elementary matrix in turn, using
          <xref ref="obs-left-multiplication" text="type-global" /> (left multiplication
          by <m>E</m> performs the corresponding row operation) together with
          <xref ref="thm-row-ops-determinant" text="type-global" />.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(I)</term> If <m>E</m> represents exchange of two rows, then
          <m>\det E = -1</m>. Also, <m>\det(EM) = -\det M</m>, since the product
          <m>EM</m> is equivalent to exchange of two rows. Hence
          <m>\det(EM) = \det E \det M</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(II)</term> If <m>E</m> represents the multiplication of the <m>i</m>th
          row by a constant <m>k</m>, then <m>\det E = k</m> and
          <m>\det(EM) = k \det M = \det E \det M</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(III)</term> If <m>E</m> represents adding <m>k</m> times row <m>i</m> to
          row <m>j</m>, then <m>\det E = 1</m> and
          <m>\det(EM) = \det M = \det E \det M</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-det-product">
      <title>Determinant of a Product</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> be <m>n \times n</m> matrices. Then
          <md>
            \det(AB) = \det A \times \det B.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          <term>Case (I): <m>A</m> is not invertible.</term>
          If <m>A</m> is not invertible, then <m>\det A = 0</m>. Also, if <m>A</m> is not
          invertible, then <m>AB</m> is not invertible by
          <xref ref="eg-noninvertible-product" text="type-global" />, and so
          <m>\det(AB) = 0</m>. Hence, in this case,
          <m>\det(AB) = 0 = \det A \det B</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>Case (II): <m>A</m> is invertible.</term>
          Hence <m>A</m> can be written as a product of elementary matrices,
          <md>
            A = E_1 E_2 \cdots E_p,
          </md>
          and <m>\det(AB) = \det \left( E_1 E_2 \cdots E_p B \right)</m>. Next, we can use
          <xref ref="eg-det-elementary-product" text="type-global" /> repeatedly to write
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \det(AB) \amp = \det E_1 \, \det \left( E_2 \cdots E_p B \right)
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = \det E_1 \, \det E_2 \, \det \left( E_3 \cdots E_p B \right)
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \;\; \vdots
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = \det E_1 \, \det E_2 \, \cdots \, \det E_p \, \det B.
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Now run the same identity backwards to reassemble <m>A</m>:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \det(AB)
              \amp = \det \left( E_1 E_2 \right) \det E_3 \cdots \det E_p \, \det B
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \;\; \vdots
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = \det \left( E_1 E_2 \cdots E_p \right) \det B
              = \det A \, \det B.
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>

    <corollary xml:id="cor-det-inverse">
      <title>Determinant of an Inverse</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>A</m> is invertible, then
          <m>\det A^{-1} = \dfrac{1}{\det A}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Since <m>A A^{-1} = I</m>, then
          <md>
            \det \left( A A^{-1} \right) = \det A \, \det A^{-1} = \det I = 1,
          </md>
          hence
          <md>
            \det A^{-1} = \frac{1}{\det A}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </proof>
    </corollary>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-det-transpose">
      <title>Determinant of a Transpose</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>A</m> is an <m>n \times n</m> matrix, then the determinant of its
          transpose is given by
          <md>
            \det(A^T) = \det A.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-det-computations">
    <title>Putting the Rules Together</title>

    <example xml:id="eg-det-big-expression">
      <title>A Determinant of a Complicated Product</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose <m>A</m>, <m>B</m> and <m>C</m> are <m>4 \times 4</m> matrices with
          <m>\det A = -1</m>, <m>\det B = 2</m>, and <m>\det C = 1</m>. Find
          <m>\det \left( 2A^2 (B^{-1}) (C^T)^3 B (A^{-1}) \right)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \det \left( 2A^2 (B^{-1}) (C^T)^3 B (A^{-1}) \right)
              \amp = \left( 2^4 \right) \det(A^2) \, \det(B^{-1}) \, \det \left( (C^T)^3 \right)
                    \det(B) \, \det(A^{-1})
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = \left( 2^4 \right) \left( \det A \right)^2
                    \frac{1}{\det B} \left( \det C \right)^3 \det B \frac{1}{\det A}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = \left( 2^4 \right) (1) \left( \frac{1}{2} \right) (1) (2) (-1)
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = -16.
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Note where each rule was used: the factor <m>2^4</m> comes from
          <xref ref="thm-det-scalar-multiple" text="type-global" /> with <m>n = 4</m>;
          the product splits by <xref ref="thm-det-product" text="type-global" />; the
          reciprocals come from <xref ref="cor-det-inverse" text="type-global" />; and
          <m>\det(C^T) = \det C</m> by
          <xref ref="thm-det-transpose" text="type-global" />.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>
</section>
